course: medicine
lesson: 66
title: Picking teams and being picked last
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Picture the gym wall in Leeds: captains Jayden and Kai pick one by one until only Oliver is left, and Kai rolls his eyes, «Ugh, fine, we'll have Ollie, innit»; Oliver's face goes hot and his stomach seems to drop.
explain: That hot face is a blush: stress chemicals like adrenaline widen the small blood vessels in the cheeks, and a lump in the throat comes from throat muscles tightening when you try not to cry.
explain: Brain scans suggest that being left out uses some of the same brain areas as body pain, so «it hurts» is true, and the feeling passes in a few minutes, faster if you tell someone.
explain: Kid captains mostly pick their mates first, so the order shows friendships and moods, not how good you are; Arjun, new from Mumbai, was picked last in Birmingham and then bowled out three batters.
explain: The fair ways are simple: the teacher counts off «one, two, one, two», uses a list, or makes captains take turns going first, and everyone gets a go at being captain.
fact: Being picked last often makes the face go hot and the stomach feel like it drops, because stress hormones like adrenaline act on blood vessels and the gut.
fact: A lump in the throat when you hold back tears is the throat muscles tightening; it is an automatic stress reaction that fades.
fact: Brain-scan studies suggest that being left out uses some of the same brain areas as physical pain.
fact: Kid captains usually pick friends first, so the picking order says little about skill.
fact: Sometimes people are picked late simply because nobody knows them yet.
fact: Counting off («one, two, one, two») or a teacher's list makes teams quickly with no public ranking.
fact: In a «snake» pick captain A picks 1, captain B picks 2 and 3, then A picks 4 and 5, so neither captain keeps the first-pick advantage and teams come out more even.
fact: Calling out who you don't want («not him, he's mid») is a public put-down and makes your own team weaker and sadder.
fact: Leading is a skill, and children only learn it when the captain's job is shared around.
fact: Anyone on the pitch can score, defend or save the game, whatever number they were picked.
fact: Saying «I don't care» doesn't stop the hurt; telling a friend or teacher how it felt helps it settle.
fact: «Mid» is young slang for average or not good; «lekker» is South African for great; «dinnae» is Scots for don't, and «dreich» is Scots for grey, wet weather.
exercise: Oliver is picked last and his face goes hot. Why? | Stress chemicals widen blood vessels in his cheeks | He has caught a fever from the gym | The gym heating is turned up too high | His heart has stopped pumping properly
exercise: Does being picked last mean you are the worst player? | No, captains mostly pick their mates first | Yes, the order is always by skill | Yes, teachers check the order is right every time | No, it means you are the best
exercise: Priya picks her bestie Simran first, though Simran is slowest. What does this show? | Picking order often follows friendships | Simran must secretly be fastest | Captains always pick the best players first, no matter what | Priya has made a rule mistake
exercise: Mr Dlamini counts the class off «one, two, one, two». What does this do? | Makes teams with nobody left standing last | Puts all the fast kids on one side | Lets the captains pick their mates without anyone noticing | Makes the teams take longer to choose
exercise: Niamh's voice wobbles as she jokes about being last. What is happening? | Her throat tightens as she holds back tears | She is catching a sore throat | She is just putting on a funny voice to make them laugh | Her lungs have run out of air
exercise: What do brain scans suggest about being left out? | It uses some of the same areas as pain | It uses no part of the brain at all | It only affects the stomach, not the head at all | It makes the brain grow faster
exercise: Aroha is picked last, then scores the winner. What does this show? | Anyone on the pitch can change the game | The last pick is always the best player | Picking order decides who scores | Captains secretly pick the best last
exercise: Brody says loudly «not Charlie, he's mid». What is that? | A public put-down that weakens his team | Honest advice that helps Charlie improve | A normal part of fair picking | A good captain's way to pick
exercise: Arjun is new and picked last, then takes three wickets. Why was he picked last? | Nobody knew him yet | He told them he couldn't bowl | He is the weakest player there | The teacher said he must be last
exercise: In a snake pick, captain A picks 1. Who picks 2 and 3? | Captain B | Captain A again | The teacher | The last kid left
exercise: Why are snake picks fairer? | Neither captain keeps the first-pick edge | The best kids all end up together on one team | The slowest kids get to be captains | It takes twice as long to pick
exercise: Freddie is captain every single lesson. What's fairer? | Take turns being captain | Keep the best player as captain | Let the teacher be captain | Have no captain ever again
exercise: Isla says «dinnae care» and kicks the wall. What would help more? | Telling a friend or teacher how she feels | Kicking the wall harder next time | Pretending it never happened at all | Shouting at the captain who left her till last
exercise: Tyler is picked last three weeks running and says «it actually hurts». Is he being soft? | No, being left out is a real hurt | Yes, only sticks and stones can hurt | Yes, he should laugh it off | No, but he must have a real injury
exercise: 22 kids split into 2 teams. How many a side? | 11 | 12 | 10 | 22
exercise: What does «dreich» mean in Scotland? | Grey, wet weather | Really great fun outdoors | A football boot with studs | Being picked last in PE
test: «lol ollie got picked last again, he's trash innit». What's the truth? | Picking order mostly shows friendships, not skill | Last means worst, that's the rule | Ollie should stop playing football | The captains measured everyone's speed
test: «get over it bro, being left out can't actually hurt». Right? | No, it uses some of the brain's pain system | Yes, only a kick can really hurt | Yes, feelings aren't in the brain | No, but only grown-ups feel it
test: «sir, can we just pick? it's quicker innit». What's the fair fix the teacher can use? | Count off or use a list | Let the two best kids always pick | Let the tallest kids pick first | Make the last kid sit out
test: In a cartoon the last kid picked is always useless and trips over the ball. What's the real-life version? | The last pick can be good and just unknown | The last pick is always the clumsiest | Last picks can't kick a ball | Real last picks never get the ball
test: Thando in Cape Town goes «lekker, sir!» when nobody's last. What does lekker mean here? | Great | Rubbish | Sleepy | Unfair
test: Kai in Manchester: «our kid's always captain, he's boss at it». What would happen if everyone took turns? | More kids would learn to lead | The games would stop working | Freddie would forget how to play | Nobody would ever win a match
test: «shut up and pick, it's 16 kids, 2 teams». How many each? | 8 | 6 | 16 | 9
test: Brody's mate: «saying who you don't want is just being honest, mate». Put him right. | It's a put-down, and it hurts his own team | It's fine if the kid really is slow | Honesty means saying it even louder | It's only bad if a teacher hears
test: Niamh tells her mum later how picking felt. What will probably happen to the feeling? | It settles faster once it's shared | It doubles because she talked about it | It stays exactly the same forever | It turns into a fever overnight
test: Isla kicked the wall and now her toe hurts too. What has she got now? | The same hurt feelings plus a sore toe | No more hurt feelings, the kick fixed it | A broken wall and a happy mood | A reason to be picked first next time
test: Priya's mate: «captains always pick the best, so Simran must be quick». True? | No, captains often pick friends first | Yes, the first pick is always fastest | Yes, captains test everyone's speed | No, captains always pick the slowest
exam: Identify the principal determinant of the order in which children are selected by child captains. | Friendship and mood rather than ability | Measured athletic ability | Alphabetical order of surname | The instructions of the head teacher
exam: Which of the following methods of team formation most effectively avoids a public ranking of pupils? | Allocation by counting off or by a teacher's list | Selection by the two most able pupils | Selection by the tallest pupils | Selection by the pupils who arrived first
exam: Select the response consistent with the established findings concerning social exclusion. | It engages certain regions of the brain associated with physical pain | It engages no region of the brain whatsoever | It produces sensations confined to the feet | It invariably results in physical injury
exam: In respect of a snake pick between captains A and B, state which captain makes the second and third selections. | Captain B | Captain A | Neither captain | The referee
